r lines stopped?]`; the budget (`stop-ticks`) is the\n+   caller's.\"\n+  [state server now]\n+  (let [state (custody-control-step (custody-stop state))\n+        [server lines stopped?] (stop-tick server now)]\n+    [state server lines stopped?]))\n+\n+\n ;; =============================================================================\n ;; clj: a reader thread that only appends, and one owned poller thread\n ;; =============================================================================\n@@ -1051,20 +1132,24 @@\n         once, then keep stepping until it reports `:stopped` or the bounded\n         budget runs out.  A connected client must observe `:ws/ended`, not the\n         `:ws/closed` a process exit would leave behind.  The bounded drain\n-        sleeps the base interval; it is a budget, not a cadence.\"\n-       [server w]\n+        sleeps the base interval; it is a budget, not a cadence.  The custody\n+        control plane steps on every one of its ticks (shutdown-tick); the\n+        state it reaches is durable in the progress journal, so what the\n+        budget leaves outstanding is a restart's to resend.\"\n+       [state server w]\n        (when server\n-         (loop [server (serve/stop! server)\n+         (loop [state state\n+                server (serve/stop! server)\n                 remaining stop-ticks]\n-           (let [[server' lines stopped?]\n-                 (stop-tick server (System/currentTimeMillis))]\n+           (let [[state' server' lines stopped?]\n+                 (shutdown-tick state server (System/currentTimeMillis))]\n              (doseq [line lines]\n                (println line))\n              (cond\n                stopped? nil\n                (zero? remaining) (println stop-timeout-text)\n                :else (do (wake/sleep! w tick-millis)\n-                         (recur server' (dec remaining))))))))\n+                         (recur state' server' (dec remaining))))))))\n \n      (defn poll-loop!\n        \"T